  "helpTitle": "Help & Information",
  "helpCyclePhases": "Phases of the menstrual cycle",
  "helpCyclePhasesBody": "The menstrual cycle consists of 4 main phases:\n\n1. Menstrual phase (days 1 to 5): Shedding and discharge of the uterine lining (endometrium) when implantation did not occur.\n2. Follicular phase (days 1 to 13): Maturation of an ovarian follicle under FSH stimulation and rising estrogen levels.\n3. Ovulation (around day 14 for an average 28-day cycle): Release of a mature egg triggered by an LH surge.\n4. Luteal phase (days 15 to 28): The corpus luteum secretes progesterone to prepare for a possible pregnancy. If fertilization does not occur, hormone levels drop, initiating the next cycle.",
  "helpPeriodVsOvulationTitle": "Difference between period and ovulation",
  "helpPeriodVsOvulationBody": "Menstruation (your period) is the shedding of the uterine lining when fertilization has not occurred, marking day 1 of a new cycle.\n\nOvulation occurs around mid-cycle when an ovary releases a mature egg ready for fertilization. Although frequently confused, they are two distinct biological events separated by several days.",
  "helpFertility": "Fertility and its variations",
  "helpFertilityBody": "The fertile window spans approximately 6 days per cycle:\n\n• The 5 days leading up to ovulation: sperm can survive for up to 5 days in fertile cervical mucus.\n• The day of ovulation and 24 hours after: lifespan of an unfertilized egg.\n\nFertility varies naturally between cycles and throughout life.",
  "helpCycleVariationsTitle": "Why cycle length varies",
  "helpCycleVariationsBody": "Variations of a few days between cycles are completely normal and physiological.\n\nCommon factors influencing cycle duration include:\n• Acute physical or emotional stress.\n• Sleep disruption, shift work, or jet lag.\n• Significant weight or nutritional changes.\n• Starting or discontinuing hormonal contraceptives.\n• Underlying health conditions (e.g. PCOS, thyroid conditions).",
  "helpPredictionLimitsTitle": "Limits of app predictions",
  "helpPredictionLimitsBody": "IMPORTANT: Predictions in Intima are statistical estimations based on your historical cycle data.\n\nThey do not constitute biological guarantees or a reliable standalone contraceptive method. No algorithm can predict with absolute certainty the exact day of ovulation. Never rely on this calendar to engage in unprotected intercourse if avoiding pregnancy.",
  "helpContraception": "Contraception methods",
  "helpContraceptionBody": "Multiple contraceptive options exist tailored to individual health needs:\n\n• Hormonal methods: combined or progestin-only pills, subdermal implants, transdermal patches, vaginal rings, hormonal IUDs.\n• Non-hormonal methods: copper IUD (spermicidal copper ions), condoms, diaphragms with spermicide.\n• Emergency contraception: morning-after pill (levonorgestrel or ulipristal acetate) or emergency copper IUD insertion.\n\nConsult a healthcare professional to identify the best contraceptive method for you.",
  "helpSTI": "Pregnancy prevention vs STI prevention",
  "helpSTIBody": "Warning: Hormonal contraception prevents pregnancy, but DOES NOT protect against Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs/HIV).\n\nOnly barrier methods (external and internal condoms) effectively reduce STI transmission.\nRegular screening is recommended with your partners, through medical clinics, family planning centers, or anonymous testing facilities.",
  "helpWhenToSeeDoctor": "When to consult a healthcare professional",
  "helpWhenToSeeDoctorBody": "Prompt medical evaluation (family physician, gynecologist, midwife, or emergency care) is recommended if you experience:\n\n• Acute, severe pelvic pain unresponsive to common analgesics.\n• Abnormally heavy bleeding (soaking multiple pads per hour for several consecutive hours) or dizziness.\n• Unusual bleeding between periods or after sexual intercourse.\n• Abnormal vaginal discharge (unusual color, strong odor, itching, or burning).\n• Fever accompanied by pelvic pain or abnormal discharge.\n• Unexplained missed period with potential pregnancy risk.",
  "helpDisclaimerTitle": "Educational medical information",
  "helpDisclaimerBody": "Information in this app is strictly for educational and preventative purposes. It never replaces professional medical consultation, clinical diagnosis, or medical treatment.",

